<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
        <string>APPL</string>
        <key>CFBundleName</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductName}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
        <string>{{.OutputFilename}}</string>
        <!-- A real reverse-DNS identifier, not the Wails template default. That
             default expands to "com.wails.ST Reborn", WITH A SPACE, which is not
             a valid bundle identifier: Apple specifies alphanumerics, hyphens
             and periods. macOS keys a lot on this string, including the
             local-network permission below and the Gatekeeper decision, so it
             has to be well formed before the app is signed and notarized.

             Changing it makes macOS treat this as a different app, so an
             existing install is asked for local-network access once more. That
             happens anyway the moment the signing identity changes from ad-hoc
             to a real certificate, so it costs nothing extra to fix it here. -->
        <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
        <string>de.st-reborn.app</string>
        <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductVersion}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleGetInfoString</key>
        <string>{{.Info.Comments}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductVersion}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
        <string>iconfile</string>
        <!-- macOS 12 is a HARD floor, not a preference. The Go toolchain STR is
             built with resolves Security.framework symbols lazily, and one of
             them, SecTrustCopyCertificateChain, only exists from macOS 12. On an
             older system the app launches, shows its window, and then dies the
             instant it makes its first HTTPS call, which is the moment Wi-Fi
             comes up and the radio catalogue is queried:

               dyld: lazy symbol binding failed: Symbol not found:
                     _SecTrustCopyCertificateChain
               Service exited with abnormal code: 2

             Reported as "the app closes as soon as Wi-Fi is on" (2026-07-28,
             Intel Broadwell Mac). This key used to say 10.13, so macOS launched
             it and let it crash with no explanation. Declaring the real floor
             makes macOS refuse the launch and say why. -->
        <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
        <string>12.0</string>
        <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
        <string>true</string>
        <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
        <string>{{.Info.Copyright}}</string>
        <!-- Local-network privacy (macOS 15+/26): the app discovers speakers via
             mDNS/Bonjour and talks to them over HTTP on the LAN. Without these
             keys the permission prompt can silently misbehave and every probe
             fails while curl works from Terminal (#420). Template base is the
             Wails CLI v2.12.0 default (the version release.yml pins); do not use
             template funcs that version lacks, e.g. safeBundleID (v2.13+). -->
        <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
        <string>ST Reborn discovers and controls your SoundTouch speakers on your local network.</string>
        <!-- ALL FOUR types discovery/discovery.go browses, not just two. macOS
             only permits the service types listed here, so the two that were
             missing simply never resolved: speakers still running the pre-rename
             agent (_soundtouchstick._tcp) and stock speakers that announce under
             the alternate spelling (_bose-soundtouch._tcp) were invisible on a
             Mac while they showed up fine on Windows. Keep this list in step
             with ServiceType / LegacyServiceType / StockServiceType and
             StockServiceTypeAliases. -->
        <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
        <array>
            <string>_streborn._tcp</string>
            <string>_soundtouchstick._tcp</string>
            <string>_soundtouch._tcp</string>
            <string>_bose-soundtouch._tcp</string>
        </array>
        <!-- The app writes the installer onto a USB stick under /Volumes, which
             macOS 13+ gates behind a removable-volumes permission. Without this
             key the prompt appears with no explanation of what is being asked. -->
        <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
        <string>ST Reborn writes the speaker installer onto your USB stick.</string>
    </dict>
</plist>
